{"type":"text","timestamp":1794384771021,"sessionID":"ses_e365d97c7ffelqEqEaqM6zrsDt","part":{"id":"prt_1c9aab7b6001rxaRo2719DVRxK","messageID":"msg_1c9aa87b6001b7K35PC4lAGSww","sessionID":"ses_e365d97c7ffelqEqEaqM6zrsDt","type":"text","text":"<think>Typecheck passes. Wait — `npm install` said \"up to date\" but the lock file had `^19.3.0` specifiers; after changing package.json to exact versions, npm should have updated the lock. Let me verify the lock file has the exact versions now.\n\nAlso `React.FormEvent` used in SignInForm/StoreForm — with `import { useState } from \"react\"` and the `React` namespace not imported... In TSX with `jsx: react-jsx`, using `React.FormEvent` as a type requires the React namespace in scope. Since I only imported `useState`, `React` is not defined → but typecheck passed. Because @types/react declares a global `React` namespace? Yes, @types/react has `declare global { namespace JSX {...} }` in older versions; in React 19 types, the global JSX namespace was moved into React.JSX. The `React` namespace as a global: @types/react exports `export = React` and `export as namespace React` — the \"export as namespace\" makes `React` available as a global UMD namespace in scripts, but in modules, using a UMD global is an error (\"refers to a UMD global, but the current file is a module\") unless `allowUmdGlobalAccess`. However, type-only usage is allowed. TS allows UMD global access in type positions. That's why it passed.\n\nTo be safe and explicit, let me import the types: `import { useState, type FormEvent } from \"react\"`. Let me update both files.\n\nLet me check the lock file state and then update the components.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794384770998,"end":1794384771017}}}
